n comment) — one decrease-only budget per rule in `TRACKED_SUPPRESSION_RULES` (`scripts/lint-suppressions.ts`) plus a residual budget for every other rule, **partitioned so a style-suppression cleanup can't fund a new security waiver** (verified in `lint-suppressions.ts`'s header comment).\n\nCorrection to the tiering claim: it's not \"security-tier suppressions require a waiver\" as a binary. There are **four tiers** (`SUPPRESSION_TIERS` in `lint-suppressions.ts`): `security` (tenancy/authz/credential/audit — gets a per-rule budget **plus** a waiver ledger entry in `scripts/suppression-waivers.json`), `data-volume`, `observability`, `test-integrity` (each gets a per-rule budget but explicitly **no** waiver ledger — \"reviewable from the baseline diff alone\"). The previous analysis missed three of the four tiers.\n\nMissed entirely: `bun scripts/ratchet.ts --self-test` — a meta-test that proves each counter counts what its description claims. `scripts/verify.sh` runs `--self-test` before `--check` in CI.\n\nWould it work with llame's `--report-unused-disable-directives`? Yes structurally — different mechanism, same CI-flag family, no conflict. Cost estimate: llame's disable-directive volume is currently tiny (repo-wide grep: 7× `jsx-a11y/no-autofocus`, 6× `jsx-a11y/prefer-tag-over-role`, 1× `vitest/valid-expect`, **zero** `anti-slop/*`). A budget ratchet has near-zero value at this suppression volume — it's insurance against future creep, not a current gap. Building the general whole-repo-metrics framework (the valuable part) is a multi-day investment; scoping it to just \"lint-suppression budgets\" is maybe a day, given `lint-suppressions.ts`'s design is directly portable.\n\n## 2. Regression fixtures — claim WRONG on \"works today at no cost\"\n\nMechanism confirmed accurate: `scripts/lint-oxlint-fixtures.sh` runs `oxlint --report-unused-disable-directives-severity=error` against `.oxlint-plugins/__fixtures__` only. Each fixture has an intentionally-suppressed violation (`oxlint-disable-next-line <rule> -- reason`) plus \"meaningful accepted cases\" (unsuppressed — if the rule starts false-positiving on those, plain `--deny-warnings` catches it too). So it catches both false-negative (detector goes silent → directive unused) and false-positive (accepted case starts failing) regressions, essentially for free riding on a flag llame already runs.\n\n**But the \"works today at no cost\" claim is false for llame specifically.** I grepped the whole repo for `oxlint-disable.*anti-slop` — zero hits. llame has **no live suppressions for any of its 18 anti-slop rules in production code**. `--report-unused-disable-directives` only catches a regression when a directive exists to go stale; with zero directives, it provides **zero** regression protection for any anti-slop rule today, contrary to what the doc claims.\n\nCross-checked against llame's actual test mechanism (`packages/oxlint-plugin-anti-slop/rules/*.test.ts`, `oxlint/plugins-dev`'s `RuleTester`) — this is NOT the same thing as stella's fixture approach; it's closer to stella's one exception (`docs-source-policy.test.ts`, a pure-function unit test — stella's only non-fixture test among 133 rules). RuleTester gives precise, isolated, messageId-level assertions that stella's fixture mechanism can't (a fixture failure is a generic oxlint CLI error at a file:line, not a named test with a diff).\n\n**The real, measurable gap: only 6 of 18 anti-slop rules have a `.test.ts` file at all** (`no-chained-type-assertions`, `no-module-mocking`, `no-unknown-parameters`, `no-untracked-todo`, `no-vacuous-throw-assertion`, `parameter-decorator-own-line`). The other 12 (`no-conditional-empty-object-spread`, `no-known-value-widening`, `no-object-parameters`, `no-reflect-apply`, `no-reflect-get`, `no-runtime-typeof`, `no-shape-in-symbol-names`, `no-unknown-returns`, `no-unknown-type-aliases`, `no-unsafe-dictionary-type`, `no-widen-then-assert`, `require-safety-comment-for-type-assertion`) have **zero** regression protection of any kind — no test, no live suppression to go stale. `registry.check.mjs` doesn't catch this either (see #4).\n\nStella's fixture approach is cheap exactly where it would plug this: one file, one suppressed violation, \"cost\" ≈ authoring effort for 12 rules × ~15-30 min each, versus writing 12 full RuleTester suites. Worth adopting for the untested rules specifically, not as a wholesale replacement of RuleTester where it already exists (RuleTester is strictly more precise).\n\n## 3. Catalogue — claim ACCURATE, disclaimer quote verbatim-verified\n\n`.oxlint-plugins/README.md` lines 35-38, confirmed verbatim: \"The text below is a concise contract, not a claim that syntax analysis proves more than it does. A rule can prove only the shapes described in its source. Security rules that recognize local data flow say so explicitly; they do not replace authorization, runtime validation, or integration tests.\" Matches the prior doc's quote exactly.\n\nllame side: confirmed **zero catalogue exists**. `packages/oxlint-plugin-anti-slop/` has `UPSTREAM.md` (documents only the 3 local vendored patches, not the 18 rules) and per-rule `meta.docs.description` one-liners inside each rule file (not browsable, no domain grouping, no detection-boundary/honesty disclaimer). This is a clean, real gap — cost to close is low (one README, ~18 one-paragraph entries, grouped by domain like stella's) and it's the cheapest of the four to adopt.\n\n## 4. Registry check — claim ACCURATE but understates the delta\n\nRead both scripts in full. Stella's `scripts/check-oxlint-plugin-registry.ts` (194 lines) checks, per plugin file: (a) registered in `oxlint.config.ts` jsPlugins, (b) imports from `@oxlint/plugins`, (c) default export wrapped in `eslintCompatPlugin`, (d) uses `createOnce(...)` not legacy per-file `create(context)`, (e) `meta.name` matches filename, (f) has derivable rule IDs, (g) has a module-named fixture, (h) README links to it exactly once, then per