/api/src/db/AGENTS.md, apps/web/lib/api/AGENTS.md, CODING_STANDARDS.md, `.oxlintrc.json`, and `packages/oxlint-plugin-anti-slop/rules/`. Every \"measured\" number below is a real grep run just now, not an estimate.\n\nOverall calibration: stella's own README states the honest boundary up front — \"The text below is a concise contract, not a claim that syntax analysis proves more than it does. A rule can prove only the shapes described in its source.\" That holds for every rule read. ~90% of the 133 rules are unusable as-is for llame: they're closed-vocabulary matches on stella's own identifiers (`workspaceId`, `t.String()`, `UserAvatar`, Elysia/Eden, Bun-only APIs, TanStack Router, Valkey/BullMQ, S3, i18n `TranslationKey`) with zero portability. The value isn't in the code, it's in five *shapes* of detector stella uses that llame's prose invariants map onto but nothing currently checks mechanically.\n\n## F1 — Naive (non-tz) timestamp columns can silently reappear — HIGH confidence, 2 measured violations\n\nStella rules: `require-timestamptz-column` + `no-naive-timestamp-cast` + `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison` (`.oxlint-plugins/require-timestamptz-column.ts`, `no-naive-timestamp-cast.ts`, `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison.ts`).\n\n**Mechanism**: `require-timestamptz-column` bans importing/calling stock Drizzle `timestamp()` from `drizzle-orm/pg-core` anywhere outside one allowlisted file that defines a wrapper `timestamptz()` helper — \"even with `{ withTimezone: true }` passed manually, so there is exactly one way to declare a timestamp column.\" The companion rules ban `::timestamp` SQL casts and comparing a round-tripped JS `Date` against a timestamp column (Postgres stores microseconds, JS `Date` truncates to ms — silent pagination/CAS bugs).\n\n**llame's prose invariant**: `apps********S.md` — \"`temporal` says 'received', never 'current', and its payload stores the instant and the IANA zone *together*.\" The whole context-injection rail depends on timestamps meaning what they say.\n\n**Evidence — llame does NOT have a central `timestamptz()` helper.** `rg 'timestamp\\('  apps/api/src/db/schema` returns 36 call sites, every one manually repeating `{ withTimezone: true }` (or `{ mode: 'date', withTimezone: true }`). Correctness rests entirely on remembering the option object at each new column, not on the type system.\n\n**Confirmed violations (2)**: `apps/api/src/db/schema/auth.ts:27` — `emailVerified: timestamp('email_verified', { mode: 'date' })` and `apps/api/src/db/schema/auth.ts:105` — `expires: timestamp('expires', { mode: 'date' }).notNull()`. Both omit `withTimezone: true` — naive `timestamp without time zone` columns whose meaning depends on the writer's session time zone, in the auth.js-derived schema file. No current lint catches this; `drizzle-kit check` only validates the migration/journal chain, not column semantics.\n\n**Detection boundary**: `require-timestamptz-column` is a syntax/import match — a column declared via a differently-named re-export or a raw `sql` DDL statement is invisible to it. `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison`'s own header is explicit: it flags *every* operand in the dangerous position rather than inferring safety, because \"whether a `Date` round-tripped through the database cannot be known from one file\" — i.e. it is deliberately over-inclusive and would need an allowlist tuned to llame's actual cursor/CAS helpers (llame doesn't appear to have `timestampCasToken`-equivalent helpers yet, so I didn't attempt to measure this one — flag as unmeasured, not zero).\n\n## F2 — apps/web's declared generated-client import boundary is real, unenforced — HIGH confidence, 1 measured violation\n\nStella shape: `confine-redis-client.ts` (import-boundary allowlist keyed on specifier + `allowedFiles`, chosen deliberately as its own rule rather than `no-restricted-imports` override because \"an oxlint override REPLACES a rule's whole config instead of merging it\").\n\n**llame's prose invariant*