reader.cancel().catch(() => undefined)`, `response.body?.cancel().catch(...)`, `client.close().catch(...)`, `transport.close().catch(...)` — all in `apps/api/src/mcp/*.ts` and `knowledge-filesystem-io.ts`). llame's MCP code independently converged on the exact pattern stella allowlists — good ratchet baseline, zero rewrite needed to adopt this rule.\n- **1 clear violation**: `apps/web/lib/services/chat/export.ts:58` — `fetchModels().catch(() => undefined)` — a full network call's failure silently discarded, no capture/log, not a body-read or teardown.\n- **3 borderline**: `apps/api/src/compaction/compaction.service.ts:535-537` — `Promise.resolve(result.toolCalls).catch(() => [])` and two `.catch(() => null)` siblings. These wrap already-resolved values (can't actually reject), so likely defensive dead code rather than a real swallowed failure — worth a human look, not a slam-dunk.\n\n**Detection boundary**: purely syntactic on the `.catch` callback body — a `.catch(err => { /* looks like it handles err but doesn't reference it */ })` with a non-trivial-looking but still-empty-effect body would slip past, and the allowlist is receiver-*method-name* based (`cancel`/`close`/`abort`), so a differently-named teardown method needs its own allowlist entry per codebase.\n\n## F4 — Feature-module ownership boundaries (`src/queue/` \"consumed only by `runs/`\") — MEDIUM confidence, 0 measured violations today, real ratchet value\n\nStella shape: `no-facade-imports.ts` / `confine-redis-client.ts` (specifier-restricted-to-allowlisted-importers).\n\n**llame's prose invariant** (`apps********S.md` structure table): \"`src/queue/` — pg-boss. Consumed **only** by `runs/`\" and \"`src/chats/` — ...Dispatches via `RunDispatchService`; never sees queue names or payloads.\"\n\n**Evidence**: checked what `apps/api/src/chats/` imports from `runs/`. Found `chat-loop.service.ts:38` importing `stuckRunThresholdMs` (a constant) and two integration tests importing the `RunJob` type from `../runs/run-queues`. **Today this doesn't cross the stated line** — the file itself isn't `src/queue/`, and llame's rule is specifically about queue-*module* consumption, not about `runs/` re-exporting a threshold constant — so I'm reporting 0 confirmed violations, not a false one. But nothing structurally stops a future PR from importing pg-boss's `send()`/`work()` directly into `chats/`; the boundary is enforced by review only, exactly the gap this rule class exists to close. This is the cleanest \"declared, currently honored by convention, zero mechanical backstop\" case in the audit — recommend it as the top candidate to actually build, ahead of F1–F3, because it's currently 100% compliant (safe to turn on immediately, no cleanup PR required) and directly encodes a module-ownership sentence that's already written down.\n\n**Detection boundary**: same as F2 — specifier-string matching, defeated by re-exports; also can't distinguish \"importing a type for documentation\" from \"importing to bypass the dispatch service,\" so a real rule would need to decide whether type-only imports are exempt (stella's own `no-facade-imports` doesn't special-case type-only imports either).\n\n## F5 — Bounded/timeout-safe fetch (`require-fetch-timeout`) — LOW-MEDIUM confidence, weaker prose match, 0 violations\n\n`apps********S.md` names `mcp-bounded-fetch.ts` as a real module (target of the mutation-testing pilot) but I could not find a written sentence mandating \"every outbound fetch must go through it\" the way stella's rule header quotes its own CLAUDE.md doing. Measured: zero raw `fetch()` calls outside `mcp-bounded-fetch.ts` in apps/api, and zero outside `lib/fetch.ts`/generated client in apps/web — full compliance today. I'm listing this lower than F1-F4 specifically because the audit asked for rules enforcing an *already-declared* prose invariant, and this one is implicit-in-code-structure rather than written down; worth flagging to whoever owns docs/mcp-tools.md as a candidate sentence to add before building the rule.\n\n## Rules whose names overpromise relative to their AST check (explicitly flagged per your ask)\n\n- **`no-secret-in-log-sink`** — reads like general secret-leak detection; it's actually a closed 9-name identifier allowlist (`apiKey`, `accessToken`, ... `staticToken`). Its own header admits: \"an aliased secret (`const k = apiKey; JSON.stringify({ k })`) still slips past.\" llame's own `protected-values.ts` protects by *resolved value*, not by *property name* — porting this rule as-is would be strictly weaker than what llame already has, not an addition to it.\n- **`require-timestamptz-column`** — sounds like it proves timezone correctness; the detection is a naming-convention allowlist (`...At`/`..._at` suffix + 9 hardcoded legacy exceptions). A correctly-named-but-differently-spelled column, or one declared via a re-exported wrapper, is invisible. The rule's own comment says as much.\n- **`mcp-security.ts`**, **`auth-lifecycle.ts`**, **`require-audit-on-mutation`** — names suggest general security/compliance guarantees; all three are single-purpose, hardcoded-identifier matches on stella's own function/table names (`redactMcpOAuthRegistrationResponse`, `revokeOrganizationMemberAuthArtifacts`, `mcpOAuthClients`). Zero of these three transfer to llame's codebase even conceptually — llame's MCP trust model (env-token interpolation, no OAuth DCR) and org-membership model are architecturally different. Listed for completeness, not recommended.\n- **`no-async-context-enter-with`** — sounds like a general async-safety invariant; llame doesn't use `AsyncLocalStorage` at all for its RLS identity context (grepped `apps/api/src/db`, `apps/api/src/lib` — zero hits). llame's `TenantDbService` scopes `app.current_user_id` via Postgres `SET LOCAL`/`set_config(..., is_local=true)` inside the transaction itself (`tenant-db.service.ts:75`), which is actually a *stronger* isolation primitive than the Node-level ambient-context pattern this rule polices — no surface for this rule to check, and no gap either.\n\n## Ranking\n\nF4 (queue/chats module boundary) > F2 (generated-client boundary) > F1 (timestamptz) > F3 (swallowed rejections) > F5 (bounded fetch). F4 and F2 are \"turn on today, zero cleanup\" wins with direct prose quotes. F1 has the most concrete live bugs (2 naive timestamp columns) but needs a new `timestamptz()` helper built first, which is a real (small) migration-adjacent change, not just a lint rule. F3 needs one human call on the compaction.service.ts sites before enabling. I did not find anything resembling a strong RLS/tenant-isolation-specific detector in the 133 rules — stella's tenant model (workspace/organization) is app-level RBAC-shaped, not RLS-shaped, so nothing there generalizes to llame's `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` / `SECURITY DEFINER` invariants in `apps/api/src/db/AGENTS.md`; that's the one invariant class in llame's docs I looked hardest for a match on and came up empty.\n\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-invariants\" color=\"cyan\">\n{\"type\":\"idle_notification\",\"from\":\"stella-invariants\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-31T16:00:24.449Z\",\"idleReason\":\"available\"}\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-invariants\" color=\"cyan\" summary=\"Reconciled: precise runAs-anchored rule + prior F1/F2/F4\">\nBoth corrections confirmed and reconciled.\n\n**On (1) no type info**: consistent with everything I read — every stella rule I opened (27 of them) is pure AST/syntax matching (regex on template literals, `TSStringKeyword`/`TSTypeLiteral` node checks, import-specifier matching, scope-stack tracking for local variables). None call a type checker. So this doesn't invalidate any of my candidates; it does mean any candidate that *sounds* like it needs semantic proof (`no-secret-in-log-sink`, `require-timestamptz-column`) is exactly as name-and-shape-limited as I already flagged.\n\n**On (2) `no-body-ownership-ids`**: confirmed unsound as a verbatim port, and I hadn't put it in my ranked list for that reason. Your `runAs` anchor is the fix — here's the sound version built on it:\n\n## F0 (new, highest priority) — identity into `.runAs()` must trace to `@CurrentUser()`, never `@Body()`/`@Query()`/`@Param()`\n\nChecked the actual trust boundary: `apps/api/src/db/tenant-db.service.ts:55-66` — `runAs<T>(userId, fn)` sets the RLS identity via `set_config('app.current_user_id', ...)`; its own comment says \"already authenticated the request, so an empty userId reaching runAs means a[n internal bug].\" The one place identity legitimately enters the system is the `@CurrentUser()` parameter decorator — confirmed at every controller call site I sampled: `apps/api/src/runs/runs.controller.ts:79` `@CurrentUser() userId: string` → line 96 `this.tenantDb.runAs(userId, ...)`; same pattern at `apps/api/src/auth/auth.controller.ts:118,129,142,155,180,193`.\n\n84 non-test `.runAs(` call sites (your count was 88 — I excluded `*.integration.test.ts` too, difference is almost certainly test files). Breakdown by first-arg shape: 23 bare `userId`, 21 `input.userId`, 19 `ownerUserId`, 5 `job.userId`, 3 `ownerId`, 3 `input.callerId`, 2 `context.userId`, rest singletons. Every controller-layer call I sampled traces to `@CurrentUser()` — **0 measured violations today**, same \"compliant now, zero structural backstop\" shape as my earlier F4.\n\n**Precise, sound rule shape** (same technique as `require-fetch-timeout`'s local scope-stack tracking, which I already confirmed works without type info): at each *controller* method, track whether the identifier reaching `.runAs(...)`'s first argument resolves — within that same function only, one hop — to a parameter decorated `@CurrentUser()`. Flag it if it instead resolves to a parameter decorated `@Body()`/`@Query()`/`@Param()`, or to a property destructured from one. This is deliberately scoped to controllers only (single-function, no cross-file dataflow, so it 