3 (swallowed rejections) and F5 (bounded fetch) from the top tier — both still real but lower-confidence prose matches, already covered in my first message if wanted.\n\n**Final ranking: F0 > F4 > F2 > F1.** F0 is the only one that's a genuine security-boundary rule with a sound anchor; F4/F2 are architecture-boundary ratchets; F1 is a real-but-non-security correctness gap.\n\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-invariants\" color=\"cyan\">\n{\"type\":\"idle_notification\",\"from\":\"stella-invariants\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-31T16:04:55.216Z\",\"idleReason\":\"available\"}\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-practices\" color=\"blue\" summary=\"Final evidence: fixture/test shapes, ratchet baseline, foreign disables\">\n## 1. Concrete fixture vs. concrete test\n\n**Stella fixture** (`.oxlint-plugins/__fixtures__/no-nanoid.fixture.ts`, full file):\n```ts\n// Passive regression fixture for `no-nanoid/no-nanoid`.\n\n// oxlint-disable-next-line no-nanoid/no-nanoid -- fixture proves a side-effect import cannot reintroduce the removed dependency\nimport \"nanoid\";\n\nconst generatedId = Bun.********IDv7();\nconst randomBytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\n\nexport { generatedId, randomBytes };\n```\nThat's the whole mechanism: one suppressed violation, some accepted lines. `oxlint --report-unused-disable-directives-severity=error` on this directory is the entire test — no assertion library, no runner logic.\n\n**Stella's one actual `.test.ts`** (`.oxlint-plugins/__tests__/docs-source-policy.test.ts`) is NOT a RuleTester test — it's a plain `bun:test` unit test of an exported pure function (`checkDocumentationSourcePolicy`), used because that rule's logic (date math, quarantine expiry, exhaustive-classification) can't be expressed as a pass/fail fixture line. It's the sole exception among 133 rules.\n\n**llame's actual test** (`packages/oxlint-plugin-anti-slop/rules/no-chained-type-assertions.test.ts`, already quoted in my earlier message) uses `RuleTester` from `oxlint/plugins-dev`, with named `valid`/`invalid` cases and `messageId` assertions.\n\n**What each catches that the other doesn't:**\n- Fixture approach: catches \"detector went completely silent\" (unused-directive) AND \"detector now fires where it shouldn't\" (an accepted line starts failing under `--deny-warnings`). Does NOT give an isolated, named failure — a break shows as a generic oxlint CLI error at a file:line, not a labeled test.\n- RuleTester approach: precise, named, multi-case, asserts exact `messageId`/error count per case — better failure attribution and easier to review in a PR diff (it's literally the spec). Costs more to author per rule (a full file vs. one line), which is exactly why 12/18 llame rules never got one.\n- Coverage math, confirmed by grep: llame RuleTester = 6/18 rules (33%). Stella fixture = 133/133 rules (100%), all at a fixed ~10-line-per-rule cost.\n\n## 2. Ratchet: own baseline, independent of `--report-unused-disable-directives`\n\nConfirmed by reading the actual scanner. `scripts/ratchet.ts`'s header: \"one declarative table of dumb, deterministic, line-based counters — **no AST, no oxlint**.\" It imports `collectLintDirectives` from `scripts/lint-suppressions.ts:350-364`:\n```ts\nexport const collectLintDirectives = (\n  content: string,\n  file: string,\n): readonly LintDirective[] => {\n  if (!DISABLE_TOKEN.test(content)) {\n    return [];\n  }\n  const cached = directiveCache.get(file);\n  if (cached?.content === content) {\n    return cached.directives;\n  }\n  const directives = scanDirectives(content, file);\n  directiveCache.set(file, { content, directives });\n  return directives;\n};\n```\nThis is its own regex/text scanner over raw source content, cached by content hash — it does not shell out to oxlint or parse oxlint's own diagnostic output. It writes to `scripts/ratchet-baseline.json` (`BASELINE_PATH` constant, confirmed in the file header) via `--write`, and gates CI via `--check` (exit 1 only on a rise).\n\n**Answer: it needs its own baseline file, and it does NOT compose with `--report-unused-disable-directives` by consuming it** — they're two independent passes over the same comments, checking different properties: oxlint's flag asks \"is each directive currently used\" (per-file, stateless); the ratchet asks \"did the total count of a tracked rule's directives across the whole repo go up since the last commit\" (repo-wide, stateful via the baseline file). Adopting the ratchet piece would mean adding `scripts/ratchet-baseline.json` (or similar) to llame — no conflict with the existing oxlint flag, but also no reuse of it.\n\n## 3. Foreign `eslint-disable` directives — bigger than the prior doc claimed, and verified live (not dead)\n\nGrep result: **103 `eslint-disable`/`eslint-disable-next-line` occurrences across 49 files**, not the single `packages/config-interpolation` file the prior research doc named. Sample file:line evidence:\n- `packages/config-interpolation/src/interpolation.ts:248,279`\n- `apps/web/app/shell/app-sidebar/index.test.tsx:47`\n- `apps/api/src/app.setup.ts:13`\n- `apps/api/src/unknown-record.ts:13`\n- `apps/api/src/canonical-json.ts:68,72`\n- `apps/api/src/tools/runner.ts:67,163`\n- `apps/api/src/mcp/mcp-server-client.ts:113,210,242,423,455,1433` (6 in one file)\n- `apps/api/src/chats/chat-loop.integration.test.ts:21,1276`\n- `apps/api/src/identity/org-units.integration.test.ts:22-26` (5 in one file — full ESLint TS-plugin rule names: `no-unsafe-assignment`, `no-unsafe-member-access`, `no-unsafe-call`, `no-unsafe-argument`, `no-unsafe-return`)\n- ...42 more files, mostly `anti-slop/no-unknown-parameters` single-line disables plus a cluster of `@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-*` block disables at the top of `*.integration.test.ts` files.\n\n**Verified these are NOT dead comments** — I ran a controlled probe (create/lint/delete a throwaway file, `apps/api/src/__scratch_eslint_disable_probe.ts