ble near-term product decision, but it is not yet scoped or issued.\n**Verdict: hold, do not write yet.** Trigger to revisit: the day any code path lets a fetch target be selected by something other than operator config — a generic web-fetch/browse tool, or the \"Later\" remote-Workspace/external-harness work. Confidence: moderate that this stays low-value through v0.7 and the standalone-Node stage; low confidence beyond that.\n\n**A3/A4. `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison` and `require-timestamptz-column` — hypothesis CONFIRMED, and stronger than stated: this isn't just a future foundational concern, it's a LIVE bug today.**\nGrepped every Drizzle `gt/lt/gte/lte(...)` call against a timestamp-named column. Found: `apps/api/src/knowledge/knowledge-space.repository.ts:169-175` —\n```ts\ngt(knowledgeSpaces.createdAt, after.createdAt),\nand(eq(knowledgeSpaces.createdAt, after.createdAt), gt(knowledgeSpaces.knowledgeSpaceId, after.id)),\n```\n`after.createdAt` is a JS `Date` (millisecond precision) round-tripped from a prior page's row against a `timestamptz` column (microsecond precision) — this is the exact \"safe forms\" violation described in the rule's own header comment (fetched in full): the `eq()` tie-breaker can silently never match a row whose DB timestamp shares a millisecond but differs in microseconds, so keyset pagination over Knowledge Spaces can duplicate or skip rows. This is a THIRD concrete instance beyond the audit's original two (`auth.ts:27`, `auth.ts:105`), and it's the pagination-cursor failure mode the rule's comment describes almost verbatim, not a hypothetical.\nBy contrast, chat-history pagination already avoids this class entirely by using a `seq`-based integer cursor (per Leo's own memory note on the #187 windowed-history work) — proof the pattern IS avoidable in this codebase when someone thinks to.\nTrigger for rising value: ROADMAP.md:125-133, Personal Realm synchronization — \"the application protocol reconciles Chats, branches, messages, compactions, and finalized receipts\" across nodes. Cross-node LWW/ordering logic is exactly a timestamp-comparison surface, and unlike a single-node pagination bug (wrong page ordering), a cross-node truncated-timestamp comparison can silently misorder or drop conflicting edits during reconciliation — correctness-critical, not cosmetic.\n**Verdict: adopt `require-timestamptz-column` now** (it already has 2 real violations per original audit) **and write+adopt `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison` now** — it has a real violation today (the knowledge-space cursor), costs one fix, and every day it's absent is a day more pagination/cursor code can be written the same wrong way. Both must precede Personal Realm sync design, not follow it — retrofitting timestamp-comparison discipline after sync logic exists means auditing distributed conflict-resolution code for a bug class that's invisible without the rule.\n\n**A5. `require-audit-on-mutation` — hypothesis WRONG as stated; wrong pattern for llame's actual write-audit design.**\nChecked: llame has zero DB audit-log infrastructure today (`rg -i audit` over `src/db/schema` hits nothing but a comment). More importantly, ROADMAP.md:60-62 states the write surface #212 actually ships is audited a different way: \"Bind the resource identity, **commit OID**, and rendered contributions into the Run's effective-context receipt\" — Git commit history *is* the designed audit trail for Knowledge/Profile writes, not a DB audit-log row. `require-audit-on-mutation`'s pattern (flag `tx.insert/update/delete` lacking a `recordAuditEvent` call) is a mismatch for a write surface whose audit mechanism is deliberately Git, not Postgres.\n**Verdict: do not adopt.** It would need a whole audit-log feature built first, which isn't roadmapped, and the one write surface that IS roadmapped uses a different (arguably stronger — attributable, diffable) audit mechanism already designed in. Revisit only if llame ever adds direct-DB write tools bypassing Git (currently excluded — all tools are `read_only`, SPEC.md:125, and VISION.md:337 defers write-capable tools generally).\n\n**A6. `require-transaction-abort` — real correctness class, but evergreen, not roadmap-triggered.**\nChecked llame's actual transaction call sites (`tenant-db.service.ts:73-90`, `messages-repository.ts:588-609`): both already throw on failure inside `db.transaction(async (tx) => {...})` rather than returning a failure-shaped value — the safe pattern the rule requires. 0 violations, consistent with disciplined existing practice.\n**Verdict:** cheap ratchet, adopt now-ish, but it's not specifically tied to any one roadmap item — its value rises generically with write-surface *volume* (Sandbox, Git writes, any future direct-DB write tool all add transaction callbacks that could get this wrong), not with one trigger event. Lower priority than A1/A3/A4 because there's no live bug and the team's existing discipline already satisfies it.\n\n**A7. `no-swallowed-rejection` — narrow rising case worth naming, not previously flagged.**\nOriginal audit found 67 sites, mostly legitimate MCP-teardown `.catch(() => {})`. New angle: once write-tool checkpoint/dedupe semantics exist (apps********S.md:231-233, SPEC.md:121 — \"the first write-capable tool must ship checkpoint-or-dedupe semantics that prevent a retry from applying the same effect twice\"), a swallowed rejection around a checkpoint write or Git operation becomes a correctness bug, not a cosmetic one — silently losing a checkpoint write reopens exactly the double-apply hole the checkpoint exists to close. Low volume, but worth a targeted review pass (not blanket enable) at the point #212 lands its checkpoint mechanism.\n\n**A8. i18n cluster — hypothesis WRONG. This is a conflation, not a rising signal.**\nFetched `require-cached-collator`, `no-raw-locale-format`, `no-untranslated-jsx-literal`, `no-unformatted-number`, `physical-properties`. All five are bound to stella's `use-intl` framework, it