committed to for the one write surface that's actually coming.\n- **General liability, not rule-specific**: per the original audit, nkzw's 125 \"free\" rules and the 18 fixed-cost ones are now live in `.oxlintrc.json` (confirmed: `pnpm --filter root .oxlintrc.json` lines 126-336 show three nkzw tiers already merged). Any NEW custom rule proposed here should first be checked against oxlint's native rule additions in whatever version llame is on when it's written — the audit's own point #6 (\"retire a custom rule when a native one covers it\") applies with more force as the ruleset grows past 215 declared rules; a stale custom rule nobody re-checks against upstream native coverage is exactly the debt CODING_STANDARDS §2 warns against (speculative machinery with no present need).\n\n## C. Adoption sequencing\n\n| Rule | Action | When | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `no-path-prefix-containment` | **Adopt now** | Before #212 implementation branch | 0-cost today; only gates *new* containment code, must exist before that code is written |\n| `require-timestamptz-column` | **Adopt now** | Immediately | Already 2 confirmed violations (prior audit); fix is cheap; foundational before sync |\n| `no-truncated-timestamp-comparison` | **Write + adopt now** | Immediately | 1 confirmed live pagination bug found this pass (`knowledge-space.repository.ts:169`); must precede Personal Realm sync design, not follow it |\n| `require-transaction-abort` | Write, low-priority adopt | Whenever convenient, no rush | 0 violations, evergreen value, no specific trigger |\n| `no-swallowed-rejection` | **Hold — targeted review, not blanket enable** | At the point #212 ships checkpoint/dedupe semantics | Rises specifically around checkpoint-write code; blanket enable still fights 60+ legitimate MCP-teardown catches |\n| `require-safe-outbound-target` | **Defer, do not write** | Revisit if/when a generic agent-directed fetch/browse tool is proposed | No current trigger; current outbound surface is 100% operator-attested by design (SPEC.md:117-119) |\n| `require-audit-on-mutation` | **Reject** | Revisit only if llame adds non-Git direct-DB write tools | Wrong audit mechanism for the one write surface that's actually roadmapped |\n| i18n/RTL cluster (5 rules) | **Reject** | Revisit only if a multi-locale UI decision is made | No i18n framework exists or is proposed; conflates UI i18n with the actual cross-lingual-recall requirement |\n\n## D. Structural practices over time\n\nThe original audit's four structural gaps (12/18 anti-slop rules untested, no rule catalogue, registry check stops short of `.oxlintrc.json` verification, ratchet doesn't compose with `--report-unused-disable-directives`) all get *more* expensive to leave unfixed as the rule count grows, but none of them have a sharp trigger — they're linear debt, not cliff-edge. The one with a real crossover point is the **suppression ratchet's value** (§4 of the original audit, currently \"insurance against future creep... lowest priority given current suppression volume\"):\n\nCrossover signal: **the day any of A1/A3/A4 above (path containment, timestamp comparisons) starts generating legitimate `oxlint-disable` directives** — which will happen naturally once #212's Git-write code or Sandbox's mount code needs a deliberately-audited exception to the containment rule, or once sync logic needs a deliberately-audited raw-Date comparison for a narrow reason. That's also roughly when llame's suppression volume crosses from \"zero\" (current, per audit) to \"some\" for the first time in a security-relevant rule — exactly the four-tier (`security`/`data-volume`/`observability`/`test-integrity`) split `scripts/ratchet.ts` already has waiting, unused. **Recommendation: the ratchet doesn't need new work now, but the moment A1 or A4 lands and produces its first `oxlint-disable`, that's the trigger to make the ratchet's `security` tier's waiver-ledger requirement actually bite** — right now it has nothing to bite on, so its zero-cost status is real, not aspirational.\n\n## Where I diverge from the brief's hypotheses (summary)\n\n- `no-path-prefix-containment`: confirmed as pre-#212 adoption target, but it does NOT address SPEC's actual named gap (TOCTOU/hardlink race) — narrower win than implied.\n- `require-safe-outbound-target`: likely wrong — no roadmap evidence of agent-directed outbound targets; current design deliberately trusts operator config instead.\n- Timestamp rules: confirmed and *stronger* than hypothesized — found a live bug this pass, not just a foundational-later concern.\n- `require-audit-on-mutation`: wrong pattern — ROADMAP already committed to Git-commit-as-audit-trail for the relevant write surface.\n- i18n cluster: wrong — conflates UI localization (not proposed anywhere) with cross-lingual retrieval (a real but unrelated requirement).\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-future\" color=\"green\">\n{\"type\":\"idle_notification\",\"from\":\"stella-future\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-31T16:24:40.289Z\",\"idleReason\":\"available\"}\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"stella-future\" color=\"green\" summary=\"Corrected A1 + full B and C sections\">\nConfirmed your correction by reading the file directly — you're right, I was wrong. `validatePath` (knowledge-filesystem-validation.ts:61-93) rejects by component, not by prefix comparison. No `startsWith`-containment pattern exists in llame today.\n\n## Revised A1, on your terms\n\nYour reframe (\"prevents regression to the weaker shape when Sandbox introduces a second boundary\") is not overstated — if anything it undersells the case, for a reason worth naming: Knowledge's `validatePath` and Sandbox's future boundary are solving **different problems**, not the same problem twice.\n\nKnowledge validates an untrusted *relative* path string before joining it to a known root (reject `..`, absolute, control chars). Sandbox (ROADMAP.md:110-123) needs to validate whether an *already-resolved absolute* directory — a user-registered Workspace, a derived worktree — falls inside a boundary (\"A host daemon exposes only directories the user registered explicitly,\" VISION.md:200). That's the classic \"is `candidate` under `root`\" containment check, a structurally different operation than component-rejection, and there is no existing llame pattern for it to imitate correctly OR incorrectly. So the Sandbox implementer isn't at risk of \"regressing\" from a known-good pattern — they're writing this containment shape in llame for the first time, with nothing in the repo to copy from. That raises the odds of reaching for `resolved.startsWith(root)`, the obvious-looking first draft, not lowers them.\n\nOne more revision: I'd originally pinned the urgency to #212. That's wrong on a closer read — ROADMAP.md:58-59 says #212 explicitly \"**use[s] the bounded Git change path proven by the Knowledge Space**,\" i.e. it's designed to reuse the existing safe module, not write fresh containment logic. **The real deadline is Sandbox execution, not #212.** Cost to adopt is still ~0 now (0 violations, narrow AST pattern, low false-positive risk per its own source), so there's no reason to wait — but if you need to rank it against something with a real deadline, rank it against Sandbox's start, not #212's.\n\nNet: keep it in the \"adopt now\" bucket, but I'm revising the reasoning, not the verdict.\n\n## B — Rules that decay or become a liability (full)\n\n1. **i18n/RTL cluster (5 rules)** — pure debt if adopted now. No i18n framework exists or is roadmapped; enforcing calls into a runtime that doesn't exist is liability from day one, not decay over time.\n2. **`require-audit-on-mutation`** — conflicts with, not just \"premature for,\" the shipped design. ROADMAP.md:60-62 commits #212's write surface to a Git-commit-OID as the audit record. A DB-row audit-log rule adopted now would either sit unused (no `tx.insert/update/delete` audit callee exists to require) or actively push toward building a second, redundant audit mechanism for a surface that already has one designed in.\n3. **`security-guards`/`require-search-scope` (app-level tenant scoping)** — already correctly rejected in the original audit as contradicting RLS-first doctrine. Roadmap reinforces rather than reverses this, but for a new reason each side of the split: the hosted multi-tenant installation stays RLS-first (no roadmap item weakens that), while the standalone personal Node (\"Next: standalone personal Node and CLI,\" ROADMAP.md:93-108, \"operates without an account\") is **single-owner** — there is no tenant to scope a query by at all. So this rule pattern has zero home in either topology, for opposite reasons. Worth stating explicitly so nobody revisits it thinking multi-node work reopens the question.\n4. **`no-request-derived-rls-identity` (llame-original, Tier B #1) — scope liability, not obsolescence.** Its invariant (\"identity resolves to `@CurrentUser()`, never `@Body()`/`@Query()`\") is correct and durable for `apps/api`'s current hosted, session-authenticated, Postgres-RLS installation, and nothing on the roadmap weakens that for THAT codebase. But the standalone personal Node (no account, per README/VISION) is very likely a **separate package/persistence model**, not a mode flag on `apps/api`. If someone copies this rule wholesale into a new `packages/cli` or `apps/node` tree, it will either misfire (no session to trace to) or get silently disabled — a maintenance trap, not a design flaw. Flag it now as scoped-to-`apps/api` in whatever doc adopts it, so a future contributor doesn't have to rediscover the boundary.\n5. **Module-ownership boundary rules generally** (`src/queue/` ↔ `runs/`, generated-client import allowlist) — not decaying, but **multiplying**: every new package (CLI, standalone Node, eventually Android) needs its own boundary rule instance rather than inheriting the existing ones' allowlists. Budget for N new small rules over 